#speculation(title: [Microaggregates Mark Acuteness, Not Chronicity])[
*Certainty: 0.35.* The two literatures may be describing different phases of illness rather than disagreeing about one. In acute critical illness, amyloid-fibrinogen microaggregates behave as an acute-phase severity marker: present in 42.3% of 104 intensive-care patients on admission and 0% of controls, associated with sepsis, and associated with disseminated intravascular coagulation and 28-day mortality @Schofield2024microclotsDIC. In stable ambulatory post-infectious illness at 483 to 596 days, aggregate number, area, and fibrin fibre density were comparable to recovered controls @Fraser2026LCcoagulation.

The speculative claim is that aggregate burden tracks the *acuteness* of physiological stress rather than the chronicity of a disease. On that reading, a positive microclot finding signals that something acute is happening — sepsis, critical illness, possibly a severe crash — and a negative finding in stable disease is the expected result rather than a contradiction.

*Falsifiable prediction:* burden should be elevated during acute severe illness and during a severe post-exertional crash, and normal in stable disease of any duration. A severity-by-time interaction of that shape supports the framing. Burden equally elevated in stable and acute phases, or equally absent in both, refutes it.

*Consequence:* This would explain the disagreement without either side being wrong, and it would change what a positive result means. For a patient, a positive microclot test during a crash would indicate acute physiological stress, not chronic clotting pathology, and would not by itself justify anticoagulation. It also predicts that measuring aggregates in stable patients will keep producing nulls, which is a testable claim about the literature rather than about patients. (Origin: brainstorm.)
] <spec:microaggregate-acuteness-marker>
